+# Decision: Concurrency Policy for Independently Edited Visits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers sometimes correct the same visit independently, often after comparing notes offline, and their edits can reach the server days apart with no reliable signal for which submission is more authoritative. The record store must turn two or more such submissions into one current record without discarding either, and the answer fixes the contract that tablet-sync and review-export — neither yet designed — will be sized around.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gate (inherited):** every original submission must be preserved regardless of how the visit's current state is resolved. Both options below pass this gate.
+
+**Weighted criteria:**
+
+- **Risk of silent scientific miscombination** (major, turns on the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and the goal of never silently discarding or altering a submitted edit) — does an automatic step ever produce a current value no single submission actually asserted?
+- **Review workload** (moderate) — how much coordinator time per season the policy generates.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** (moderate) — the standing engineering surface the policy requires, beyond initial build.
+- **Operability by two coordinators without a dedicated systems administrator** (moderate, turns on the HLD tenet of the same name) — can the two coordinators reason about and operate the policy unassisted, including when something about it looks wrong?
+- **Submission and client-state footprint** (minor) — what tablet-sync must store and transmit per visit.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission names the revision it started from. When two submissions name the same starting revision, both are stored as competing complete revisions and a coordinator resolves the visit by picking exactly one as current. No field is ever combined across submissions by the server.
+
+- Risk of silent miscombination: **strong fit** — the server never combines fields, so there is no mechanism by which a current value could assert something no single worker submitted.
+- Review workload: **weak fit** — any two submissions sharing a base become review work, even when they touch unrelated fields (e.g. one corrects an observer name, the other adds a missing species).
+- Implementation/maintenance burden: **strong fit** — no merge-rule catalog to build or keep current as fields change.
+- Operability by two coordinators: **strong fit** — resolving a visit is "pick the right whole revision," a judgment call about complete, readable submissions rather than a judgment about a rule engine's output.
+- Submission/client footprint: **strong fit** — tablet-sync stores one base revision ID per visit and sends a flat payload; no structured diff.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission supplies its base state and its changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically into a new current revision; changes that touch the same field go to review.
+
+- Risk of silent miscombination: **weak fit** — fields that are textually disjoint but scientifically related (a species identification and its cover estimate, an observer correction that changes who the "lead observer" note refers to) can combine automatically with no coordinator ever seeing the result, because no field-name rule reliably detects that relationship.
+- Review workload: **strong fit** — only genuinely overlapping edits reach a coordinator; most independent corrections resolve themselves.
+- Implementation/maintenance burden: **weak fit** — requires and maintains combination rules per field shape (scalars, the observer list, species entries), rules that must be revisited as the visit schema changes.
+- Operability by two coordinators: **partial fit** — reviewing an actual overlap is simple, but diagnosing a bad auto-combined value after the fact requires understanding what the merge rules did, which is a different and harder kind of reasoning than reading a submission.
+- Submission/client footprint: **partial fit** — tablet-sync must retain a base snapshot and emit structured changes rather than a flat payload.
+
+Both options were sketched with the understanding that neither rules out requiring manual approval before a combined or resolved record is treated as final; the distinction is where manual attention is required, not whether it exists.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The decisive criterion is the risk of silent scientific miscombination. Field-aware merging cannot reliably tell a disjoint edit from one that happens to be scientifically entangled with another field it isn't named the same as — a species identification and its cover estimate, for instance. An automatic combination that crosses that kind of relationship produces a current value no worker actually submitted, and no coordinator would see it unless they happened to re-review an already-resolved visit. Whole-visit revisions close that risk structurally: the server never combines fields, so every current value is traceable to exactly one worker's single submission. This is a direct reading of the tenet that the path from a submitted observation to an exported value stay inspectable, and of the stronger standing goal of never silently altering what a worker asserted — not just never discarding it outright.
+
+This is chosen knowingly against the review-workload criterion: whole-visit revisions will generate review work for conflicts that field-aware merging would have resolved automatically, including plenty of conflicts that turn out, on review, to have been genuinely unrelated edits. The trade accepted here is a larger, less-triaged review queue in exchange for removing a category of error that a coordinator has no natural way to catch after the fact.
+
+**Why field-aware merging stays worth reconsidering.** The review queue is a real operating cost, and this decision does not have evidence yet about what fraction of real conflicts are safely disjoint versus scientifically entangled. If the whole-visit queue turns out to overwhelm two coordinators in practice — the operating risk this option trades toward — a narrower version of field-aware merging, scoped to fields that carry no plausible scientific relationship to any other field (for instance, observer-name corrections alone), could recover some of the review-workload benefit without reopening the risk this decision is built to avoid. That narrower version was not evaluated here and would need its own pass through this decision if proposed.
+
+### What this obligates downstream
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain each visit's current base revision ID locally, submit it with every edit, and learn from the server's response whether its submission became the current revision or a competing one — and needs a way to show that outcome to the worker who submitted it.
+- **Review-export** must build a review queue keyed on visits with competing revisions, present competing revisions as full, readable alternatives for a coordinator to compare (not a field-level diff), implement the resolution call that records a coordinator's pick, and exclude visits with unresolved competing revisions from the Friday export until resolved.
+
+Neither component's design is settled by this decision beyond those obligations; both still own how they meet them.
